
  <sn><md>### Radioactive Material

A **radioactive material** is a substance that contains **unstable atomic nuclei** that spontaneously undergo **radioactive decay**, releasing ionizing radiation in the form of **alpha ($\alpha$), beta ($\beta$), or gamma ($\gamma$) radiation**.

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Enviromental%20Pollution%20and%20Mitigation/02.Key%20Definitions/assets/alpha_beta_gamma_daigram.jpeg)

**Why are radioactive materials unstable?**

Atoms contain:

* **Protons** — positively charged
* **Neutrons** — electrically neutral
* **Electrons** — negatively charged

Some atomic nuclei have an unfavorable **proton-to-neutron ratio** or excess nuclear energy. Such nuclei are unstable and transform into more stable nuclei by releasing radiation.

```text
Unstable nucleus
       ↓
Radioactive decay
       ↓
Radiation released
       ↓
More stable nucleus
```

Types of radioactive radiation

| Radiation            | Nature                           | Penetration | Example               |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------- | ----------- | --------------------- |
| **Alpha ($\alpha$)** | 2 protons + 2 neutrons           | Very low    | Radium, uranium decay |
| **Beta ($\beta$)**   | High-energy electron or positron | Moderate    | Strontium-90          |
| **Gamma ($\gamma$)** | Electromagnetic radiation        | Very high   | Cobalt-60             |
